1
00:00:03,699 --> 00:00:06,881
On October second, this low-water crossing near Fort Hood,

2
00:00:06,881 --> 00:00:09,888
Texas, went from dry to more than seven feet under

3
00:00:09,888 --> 00:00:12,199
water in about six hours.

4
00:00:12,199 --> 00:00:15,368
Over half of flood drownings happen when someone drives into

5
00:00:15,368 --> 00:00:16,645
water like this.

6
00:00:16,645 --> 00:00:19,071
RiverWatch watches crossings like this one,

7
00:00:19,071 --> 00:00:21,793
through cameras that already exist.

8
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:22,739
The U.S.

9
00:00:22,739 --> 00:00:26,732
Geological Survey runs more than thirteen hundred river cameras,

10
00:00:26,732 --> 00:00:29,066
and nobody measures their images.

11
00:00:29,066 --> 00:00:31,690
Gages fail exactly when they matter.

12
00:00:31,690 --> 00:00:34,499
Right now, the official gage at this crossing has a

13
00:00:34,499 --> 00:00:38,249
silent primary sensor and a backup stuck at six point

14
00:00:38,249 --> 00:00:42,098
two feet, while the camera shows a dry creek.

15
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:45,779
Everything visual is classical OpenCV 5,

16
00:00:45,779 --> 00:00:47,450
with no trained network.

17
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:50,933
A quality gate rejects infrared night frames,

18
00:00:50,933 --> 00:00:52,408
blur and fog.

19
00:00:52,408 --> 00:00:57,016
ECC registration aligns each frame using only the bridge and

20
00:00:57,016 --> 00:00:57,922
the banks.

21
00:00:57,922 --> 00:01:02,891
A dense normalized cross-correlation between frames fifteen minutes apart shows

22
00:01:02,891 --> 00:01:03,959
what moved:

23
00:01:03,959 --> 00:01:06,026
flowing water changes everywhere,

24
00:01:06,026 --> 00:01:07,651
concrete does not.

25
00:01:07,651 --> 00:01:10,380
Each water body must be dense and smooth,

26
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:13,642
which rejects sunlit gravel under a moving shadow.

27
00:01:13,642 --> 00:01:16,150
The top edge of the water across the channel becomes

28
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:17,717
a virtual staff gauge,

29
00:01:17,717 --> 00:01:20,648
calibrated against the official gage.

30
00:01:20,949 --> 00:01:25,535
We evaluated on almost four thousand frames with cross-validation by

31
00:01:25,535 --> 00:01:29,030
day, so the flood day was never used for calibration.

32
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:32,756
When water flows, the camera is within twenty centimetres on

33
00:01:32,756 --> 00:01:36,692
average, and eighty-seven percent of readings are within one foot.

34
00:01:36,692 --> 00:01:38,073
For the question that matters,

35
00:01:38,073 --> 00:01:40,186
is the crossing under a foot of water,

36
00:01:40,186 --> 00:01:42,990
precision is ninety-seven percent.

37
00:01:43,290 --> 00:01:46,877
An Amazon Nova agent on Bedrock decides what to do

38
00:01:46,877 --> 00:01:50,163
next. This is a replay of October second on a

39
00:01:50,163 --> 00:01:51,626
simulated clock.

40
00:01:51,626 --> 00:01:55,190
At nine in the morning the upstream gage starts rising,

41
00:01:55,190 --> 00:01:58,615
and the agent switches from hourly checks to every fifteen

42
00:01:58,615 --> 00:02:02,342
minutes. At ten twenty-five the water covers the crossing.

43
00:02:02,342 --> 00:02:04,478
The agent asks to close it,

44
00:02:04,478 --> 00:02:07,671
but a guardrail in code blocks the request:

45
00:02:07,671 --> 00:02:10,864
escalation needs two independent readings.

46
00:02:10,864 --> 00:02:12,907
It asks for caution instead,

47
00:02:12,907 --> 00:02:14,614
and fifteen minutes later,

48
00:02:14,614 --> 00:02:16,704
with camera and gage agreeing,

49
00:02:16,704 --> 00:02:20,001
it asks the duty operator to close the crossing.

50
00:02:20,001 --> 00:02:22,590
Nothing reaches the public without a human.

51
00:02:22,590 --> 00:02:24,436
When the camera misses water,

52
00:02:24,436 --> 00:02:27,280
the agent looks at the frame itself and flags the

53
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:28,505
camera.

54
00:02:28,823 --> 00:02:31,029
It runs live on AWS.

55
00:02:31,029 --> 00:02:35,522
A Lambda function on Graviton runs OpenCV and the agent

56
00:02:35,522 --> 00:02:39,655
every fifteen minutes, S3 and CloudFront serve this page,

57
00:02:39,655 --> 00:02:42,430
and SNS notifies the operator.

58
00:02:42,430 --> 00:02:45,251
Today it reports the crossing as dry and the official

59
00:02:45,251 --> 00:02:49,796
gage as broken, instead of raising a false alarm.

60
00:02:50,096 --> 00:02:52,081
It works on one camera so far,

61
00:02:52,081 --> 00:02:55,367
in daylight, with a quick calibration per site.

62
00:02:55,367 --> 00:02:57,132
The limits are in the report.

63
00:02:57,132 --> 00:02:59,442
But the cameras are already there.

64
00:02:59,442 --> 00:03:02,449
RiverWatch turns them into a second pair of eyes for

65
00:03:02,449 --> 00:03:03,917
every crossing.

